From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:41:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: Keep the video PLL VCO within its range

The A733 manual gives PLL_VIDEO0, PLL_VIDEO1 and PLL_VIDEO2 a VCO range
of 1.26 GHz to 2.52 GHz (section 4.1.3.2, Table 4-2). The VCO is
24 MHz * N / M1, before the output dividers P0 and P1 (sections
4.1.6.21, 4.1.6.26 and 4.1.6.31). The driver limits only N, so the rate
search takes the lowest N and M1 that give the best rate, wherever that
puts the VCO. Any consumer that sets a rate through a video PLL runs
that search: the pixel clock of a DisplayPort monitor does, once edp-tv
passes its rate request on to the parent.

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S: 1920x1200 at 154 MHz on the DisplayPort
left PLL_VIDEO0 at N 77 and M1 2 (VCO 924 MHz), 1024x768 at 65 MHz at
N 65 and M1 2 (780 MHz). The PLL locked and the picture was stable, but
the VCO was outside the range.

Set min_rate and max_rate of the three PLLs to the range.
ccu_nm_determine_rate() answers a request outside it with the nearest
end, and inside it the search cannot leave the range: both ends are
rates the PLL makes, 1260 MHz with N 105 and M1 2, 2520 MHz with N 105
and M1 1. M1 stays free. Fixed at 1, as for PLL_GPU0, it would put the
VCO on a 24 MHz grid, and the search would miss some pixel clocks next
to a multiple of 24 MHz by more than the 0.5 % VESA DMT allows.

Not covered: PLL_DDR, PLL_PERI0, PLL_PERI1, PLL_VE0, PLL_VE1, PLL_NPU
and PLL_DE have the same range and are not limited here. PLL_GPU0 and
PLL_AUDIO0 are limited by their own patches, PLL_AUDIO1 (1.8 GHz to
3.1 GHz) is not. A video PLL that the boot chain left outside the range
keeps its rate until a request changes it.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on one board with a DisplayPort
monitor, in two modes; PLL_VIDEO0 locked with its VCO within the range
in both, and the CRC that the sink computes for a white picture was the
same as without the patch.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
index 5c8420fbf3bd..416c7f98aa1b 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -255,12 +255,21 @@ static struct ccu_nkmp pll_gpu0_clk = {
 	},
 };
 
+/*
+ * The VCO of the video PLLs, 24 MHz * N / M1, runs from 1.26 GHz to
+ * 2.52 GHz. The PLL can make both ends: N is 105 with M1 at 2 and at 1.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MIN	1260000000U
+#define SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MAX	2520000000U
+
 #define SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO0_REG	0x120
 static struct ccu_nm pll_video0_clk = {
 	.lock		= BIT(28),
 	/* N = PLL_N + 1, PLL_N 11 to 254 */
 	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
 	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1), /* input divider */
+	.min_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MIN,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MAX,
 	.common		= {
 		.reg		= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO0_REG,
 		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-video0", pll_ref_hws,
@@ -285,6 +294,8 @@ static struct ccu_nm pll_video1_clk = {
 	/* N = PLL_N + 1, PLL_N 11 to 254 */
 	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
 	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1), /* input divider */
+	.min_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MIN,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MAX,
 	.common		= {
 		.reg		= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO1_REG,
 		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-video1", pll_ref_hws,
@@ -309,6 +320,8 @@ static struct ccu_nm pll_video2_clk = {
 	/* N = PLL_N + 1, PLL_N 11 to 254 */
 	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
 	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1), /* input divider */
+	.min_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MIN,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO_VCO_MAX,
 	.common		= {
 		.reg		= SUN60I_A733_PLL_VIDEO2_REG,
 		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-video2", pll_ref_hws,
